Summary (approach)
- I'll deduplicate thumbnails by computing a content hash (SHA-256) of the thumbnail bytes. This is reliable and efficient for duplicate detection.
- I'll store one copy of the thumbnail bytes per unique hash, and maintain a mapping from file -> hash and hash -> set of files that reference it.
- I'll use thread-safe collections (ConcurrentHashMap and concurrent key sets) so the method is safe when called concurrently.
- The InputStream is fully consumed and closed here; any IO errors are propagated as IOException.

Implementation
Add these static fields to the class (one-time, at class level):

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

private static final ConcurrentMap<String, byte[]> THUMB_CACHE = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
private static final ConcurrentMap<String, Set<String>> HASH_TO_FILES = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
private static final ConcurrentMap<String, String> FILE_TO_HASH = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
```

Then implement cacheReceiveThumb as follows:

```java
/**
 * Caches the received thumbnail image.
 * @param fileInfo the information about the file
 * @param inputStream the input stream containing the thumbnail image data
 * @throws IOException if an I/O error occurs
 */
public static void cacheReceiveThumb(BaseFileInfo fileInfo, InputStream inputStream) throws IOException {
    if (fileInfo == null || inputStream == null) {
        return;
    }

    // Build a stable file key for mapping - adjust to actual BaseFileInfo getters if different
    String fileKey = (fileInfo.getName() != null ? fileInfo.getName() : fileInfo.toString())
            + ":" + fileInfo.getSize();

    // Read stream and compute hash while writing bytes to a buffer
    ByteArrayOutputStream baos = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
    MessageDigest md;
    try {
        md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
    } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
        // SHA-256 should always be present; wrap as IOException if not
        throw new IOException("Unable to get SHA-256 MessageDigest", e);
    }

    byte[] buffer = new byte[8192];
    int read;
    try {
        while ((read = inputStream.read(buffer)) != -1) {
            md.update(buffer, 0, read);
            baos.write(buffer, 0, read);
        }
    } finally {
        // ensure underlying stream is closed by this method
        try {
            inputStream.close();
        } catch (IOException ignore) { /* ignore close error */ }
    }

    byte[] thumbBytes = baos.toByteArray();
    if (thumbBytes.length == 0) {
        // nothing to cache
        return;
    }

    String hash = bytesToHex(md.digest());

    // Deduplicate using the hash:
    // - If hash already present, just register file -> hash mapping and add file to ref set
    // - If new hash, store bytes and register file
    byte[] existing = THUMB_CACHE.putIfAbsent(hash, thumbBytes);
    // Ensure file -> hash mapping and hash -> files set are updated
    FILE_TO_HASH.put(fileKey, hash);
    Set<String> fileSet = HASH_TO_FILES.computeIfAbsent(hash, k -> ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet());
    fileSet.add(fileKey);

    // If an existing value was present, we dropped the duplicate thumbBytes in favor of the stored one.
    // No further action required.
}

/* helper */
private static String bytesToHex(byte[] bytes) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(bytes.length * 2);
    for (byte b : bytes) {
        sb.append(String.format("%02x", b & 0xff));
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

Notes and optional improvements
- If BaseFileInfo exposes a unique ID (e.g., getId()), prefer using that for fileKey instead of name:size to avoid collisions.
- If thumbnails can be large and keeping them fully in memory is a concern, store them to disk or a blob store keyed by hash rather than byte[] in memory. The same hash-based deduplication applies; replace THUMB_CACHE byte[] with a mapping to a file path or storage key.
- Reference counting: if you want to remove unused thumbnails later, maintain reference counts or remove fileKey from HASH_TO_FILES set and when the set becomes empty, remove the hash from THUMB_CACHE and delete the stored data.
- If stream is used elsewhere or shouldn't be closed here, remove the close in finally and document that caller is responsible for closing. Currently the implementation closes it.

Goal recap: change the given array and produce a script that calculates the sum of the array's second and third elements (remembering JavaScript arrays are zero-indexed: second = index 1, third = index 2).

Recommendation (simple, robust, production-ready):

1) Altered array (example):
const array = [10, 20, 30, 40];

2) Minimal script to compute and print the sum:
const array = [10, 20, 30, 40];
const sum = array[1] + array[2];
console.log(sum); // prints 50

3) Robust function with validation (safer for real use):
const array = [10, 20, 30, 40];

function sumSecondAndThird(arr) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) throw new TypeError('Input must be an array');
  if (arr.length < 3) throw new RangeError('Array must contain at least 3 elements');
  const second = Number(arr[1]);
  const third = Number(arr[2]);
  if (!Number.isFinite(second) || !Number.isFinite(third)) {
    throw new TypeError('Second and third elements must be numeric');
  }
  return second + third;
}

console.log(sumSecondAndThird(array)); // prints 50

Why this approach:
- Uses clear indexing (index 1 and 2) to avoid ambiguity.
- The robust function enforces input correctness and converts/checks numeric values, reducing runtime errors in production.
- The altered array [10, 20, 30, 40] is illustrative — replace with your real data as needed.

If you want this in a different language, or to handle more cases (e.g., skip non-numeric values, support sparse arrays, return null instead of throwing), tell me which behavior you prefer and I'll adapt the implementation.

Below is a clear, idiomatic PHP translation of your Python function, with explanatory comments added throughout. I also include a short explanation of the logic, complexity, and a couple of practical suggestions at the end.

PHP code (with comments):

```php
<?php

/**
 * Check if a non-negative integer is prime.
 *
 * @param int $n  A non-negative integer (we call this with abs values).
 * @return bool   True if $n is prime, false otherwise.
 */
function is_prime(int $n): bool
{
    // Numbers less than 2 are not prime
    if ($n < 2) {
        return false;
    }

    // Only test divisors up to sqrt(n)
    $limit = (int) floor(sqrt($n));
    for ($i = 2; $i <= $limit; $i++) {
        // If any divisor divides evenly, $n is composite
        if ($n % $i === 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    // No divisor found -> prime
    return true;
}

/**
 * Recreates the behavior of the Python prod_signs function:
 * - Identifies unique values from $arr whose absolute value is prime
 * - If there are no such primes, returns null
 * - For each such prime value x:
 *     - if x appears an even number of times in $arr, add x to the sum
 *     - if x appears an odd number of times in $arr, add -x to the sum
 *
 * @param array $arr  Array of integers
 * @return int|null   Computed sum, or null if no prime-valued elements are found
 */
function prod_signs(array $arr): ?int
{
    // If input is empty, nothing to do: match Python behavior (no primes -> null)
    if (empty($arr)) {
        return null;
    }

    // Build a frequency map for array elements (O(n)).
    // This avoids repeated array scans when checking counts.
    $counts = array_count_values($arr); // keys are values from $arr, values are counts

    // Get the unique elements (the keys of the counts map)
    $unique = array_keys($counts);

    // Collect unique values whose absolute value is prime
    $primes = [];
    foreach ($unique as $val) {
        // Use abs($val) so negative primes like -3 are treated as prime checks
        if (is_prime(abs($val))) {
            $primes[] = $val;
        }
    }

    // If there are no prime-valued elements, return null (like Python's None)
    if (empty($primes)) {
        return null;
    }

    // Sum according to parity-of-count rule:
    // - if count is even -> add the original value ($p)
    // - if count is odd  -> add the negation (-$p)
    $sum = 0;
    foreach ($primes as $p) {
        $count = $counts[$p] ?? 0;
        if ($count % 2 === 0) {
            $sum += $p;   // even occurrences -> keep sign
        } else {
            $sum += -$p;  // odd occurrences -> invert sign
        }
    }

    return $sum;
}
```

Notes, complexity, and practical suggestions
- Time complexity: O(n) to build counts + O(u * sqrt(maxAbs)) to check primality for each unique element (u = number of unique values). This is more efficient than repeatedly counting occurrences (which would be O(n * u)).
- Behavior: We use abs() when testing primality so negative primes are recognized (matching the Python code). Unique values are considered as distinct by sign (3 and -3 are distinct elements).
- Return value: Follows Python behavior exactly — returns null when no primes are present.
- Possible improvements:
  - If input contains large numbers or many unique values, consider a sieve (precompute primes up to max abs value) for better performance.
  - If you want 3 and -3 to be treated as the same prime (aggregate counts across signs), change the keys to use abs() when building the counts map.
  - Add type checks or casting if input might contain non-integer values.

If you want, I can:
- Provide a version that treats +p and -p as the same prime (aggregate counts by absolute value), or
- Implement a Sieve of Eratosthenes variant when max absolute value is large.

Good—there are several practical ways to add a tooltip to that SVG depending on how the SVG is included and how fancy you want the tooltip to be. Below I give a robust, accessible, and practical solution that:

- works when the SVG is inline in the HTML (recommended),
- shows a styled HTML tooltip that follows the pointer,
- supports keyboard focus (tab, Esc),
- supports touch (tap to toggle),
- and is ARIA-friendly.

If your SVG is loaded via <img src="..."> it cannot be scripted. Use inline SVG or <object> for full interactivity.

Full example (copy/paste into an HTML file):

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1" />
  <title>SVG Tooltip Example</title>
  <style>
    /* container keeps SVG positioned for absolute tooltip placement */
    .svg-wrap {
      display: inline-block;
      position: relative;
      padding: 8px;
      background: #fff;
    }

    /* tooltip styles */
    .tooltip {
      position: absolute;
      pointer-events: none; /* ignore pointer so it doesn't block events */
      background: rgba(0,0,0,0.85);
      color: white;
      font-size: 13px;
      padding: 6px 8px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      white-space: nowrap;
      transform: translate(-50%, -120%); /* place above pointer by default */
      transition: opacity 120ms ease, transform 120ms ease;
      opacity: 0;
      z-index: 1000;
    }
    .tooltip.visible {
      opacity: 1;
      pointer-events: auto;
    }
    /* little arrow */
    .tooltip::after {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      left: 50%;
      bottom: -6px;
      transform: translateX(-50%);
      border-width: 6px 6px 0 6px;
      border-style: solid;
      border-color: rgba(0,0,0,0.85) transparent transparent transparent;
    }

    /* make the SVG interactive element focusable outline */
    .interactive {
      cursor: pointer;
      outline: none;
    }
    .interactive:focus-visible {
      box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(79, 144, 255, 0.5);
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
