  <script>
    (function () {
      const wrap = document.getElementById('svgWrap');
      const shape = document.getElementById('interactiveShape');
      const tooltip = document.getElementById('svgTooltip');

      let visible = false;
      let touchToggle = false;

      function showTooltip(text) {
        tooltip.textContent = text;
        tooltip.classList.add('visible');
        tooltip.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');
        visible = true;
      }
      function hideTooltip() {
        tooltip.classList.remove('visible');
        tooltip.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
        visible = false;
        touchToggle = false;
      }

      function positionTooltip(clientX, clientY) {
        const wrapRect = wrap.getBoundingClientRect();
        // position relative to wrapper
        let left = clientX - wrapRect.left;
        let top = clientY - wrapRect.top;
        // default above pointer
        tooltip.style.left = `${left}px`;
        tooltip.style.top = `${top}px`;

        // keep tooltip in bounds horizontally
        const tRect = tooltip.getBoundingClientRect();
        const overflowRight = tRect.right - (wrapRect.right);
        const overflowLeft = wrapRect.left - tRect.left;
        if (overflowRight > 0) {
          tooltip.style.left = `${left - overflowRight}px`;
        } else if (overflowLeft > 0) {
          tooltip.style.left = `${left + overflowLeft}px`;
        }

        // if it would go above the wrapper top, place under pointer
        if (tRect.top < wrapRect.top) {
          tooltip.style.transform = 'translate(-50%, 8px)'; // place below
          tooltip.style.top = `${top}px`;
        } else {
          tooltip.style.transform = 'translate(-50%, -120%)'; // above
        }
      }

      // hover
      shape.addEventListener('mouseenter', (e) => {
        showTooltip('This is a helpful tooltip');
        positionTooltip(e.clientX, e.clientY);
      });
      shape.addEventListener('mousemove', (e) => {
        if (visible && !touchToggle) positionTooltip(e.clientX, e.clientY);
      });
      shape.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {
        if (!touchToggle) hideTooltip();
      });

      // keyboard support
      shape.addEventListener('focus', (e) => {
        // position near center of shape bounding box
        const r = shape.getBoundingClientRect();
        const cx = r.left + r.width / 2;
        const cy = r.top + r.height / 2;
        showTooltip('This is a helpful tooltip');
        positionTooltip(cx, cy);
      });
      shape.addEventListener('blur', hideTooltip);
      shape.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
        if (e.key === 'Escape') hideTooltip();
      });

      // touch support: tap shape to toggle tooltip
      shape.addEventListener('touchstart', (e) => {
        e.preventDefault(); // avoid simulated mouse events in many browsers
        touchToggle = !touchToggle;
        if (touchToggle) {
          const t = e.touches[0];
          showTooltip('This is a helpful tooltip');
          positionTooltip(t.clientX, t.clientY);
        } else {
          hideTooltip();
        }
      }, {passive: false});

      // tap outside -> hide
      document.addEventListener('touchstart', (e) => {
        if (!wrap.contains(e.target)) hideTooltip();
      });

      // click outside with mouse hides
      document.addEventListener('mousedown', (e) => {
        if (!wrap.contains(e.target)) hideTooltip();
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>

Notes and trade-offs
- Inline SVG required: If your SVG is in an external file and inserted as <img>, you cannot attach events to inner shapes. Use inline SVG or embed via <object> or <iframe>.
- Simple alternative: Add a <title> inside the SVG (you already have one in the example). That produces an OS/browser tooltip automatically on hover, but styling and positioning are controlled by the browser and it's not as flexible. It is accessible by default.
- Accessibility: The example sets aria-describedby on the shape and role="tooltip" on the tooltip element, plus keyboard focusability (tabindex="0"). You can improve it further by programmatically managing aria-expanded or toggling aria-hidden based on your needs.
- Performance: For many identical icons, consider creating a single tooltip element and reusing it (like in the example). That avoids creating many DOM nodes.
- Visual polish: Add animations, smarter collision detection, or snap-to-shape center for keyboard focus.

If you want a shorter solution (pure SVG <title>), or a version using <foreignObject> to keep the tooltip inside the SVG (no external HTML), tell me which environment you’re deploying in (inline vs <img> vs React/Angular) and I’ll give the exact pattern optimized for that environment.

Summary (recommended solution)
- Use a two-pointer greedy scan in O(n1 + n2) time and O(1) extra space.
- Maintain i (index into nums1) and j (index into nums2). Move j forward whenever nums1[i] <= nums2[j] to try to increase distance; otherwise move i forward because no later j will satisfy nums1[i] <= nums2[j].
- This exploits the non-increasing property of both arrays and yields the correct maximum j - i.

Reasoning / correctness (concise)
- Invariant: pointers only move to the right (never decrease). This is safe because arrays are non-increasing:
  - If nums1[i] > nums2[j], then for any j' >= j, nums2[j'] <= nums2[j] so nums1[i] > nums2[j'] — no j' will work for this i; therefore increment i.
  - If nums1[i] <= nums2[j], j is a valid match for i; increasing j might give a larger distance, so increment j to try to improve j - i.
- Because i and j only advance, each element is visited at most once, so time is O(n1 + n2). Space is O(1).
- If no valid pair exists we return 0 (ans remains 0).

Scala implementation (two-pointer)
```
object Solution {
  def maxDistance(nums1: Array[Int], nums2: Array[Int]): Int = {
    var i = 0
    var j = 0
    var best = 0
    val n1 = nums1.length
    val n2 = nums2.length

    while (i < n1 && j < n2) {
      if (nums1(i) <= nums2(j)) {
        best = math.max(best, j - i)
        j += 1
      } else {
        i += 1
      }
    }

    best
  }

  // Example quick test
  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {
    println(maxDistance(Array(55,30,5,4,2), Array(100,20,10,10,5))) // 2
    println(maxDistance(Array(2,2,2), Array(10,10,1)))             // 1
    println(maxDistance(Array(30,29,19,5), Array(25,25,25,25,25))) // 2
  }
}
```

Alternative approach
- For each i, binary-search the rightmost j in nums2 (j >= i) such that nums2[j] >= nums1[i]. That yields O(n1 log n2) time. Simpler to reason about, but slower asymptotically than the two-pointer O(n1 + n2) approach.

Tradeoffs and when to use
- Two-pointer: best in practice when both arrays are large and already non-increasing (as given).
- Binary search: useful if you prefer simpler per-i logic or if nums2 were not monotonic but you had a different preprocessing — but not necessary here.

If you want, I can also provide the binary-search Scala version or annotate the code with step-by-step execution for a specific input.

Summary (expert view)
- Core idea: explore the unknown, hidden grid by backtracking DFS to build an explicit graph of reachable cells (while physically moving the robot). Record the cost to enter each neighbor as you move. After the whole reachable region is mapped and you have the coordinates of the target (if reachable), run Dijkstra on the constructed graph to compute the minimum total cost from the start to the target.
- Why this works: you must physically move the robot to discover neighbors and costs. By always backtracking to the previous cell after exploring a branch you guarantee the robot returns so the exploration is correct and complete. The movement cost model (“cost to enter a cell”) fits neatly into directed edge weights: an edge from A to B has weight equal to the cost returned when moving into B.
- Risk & correctness: we only use canMove before attempting moves so we never try invalid moves. The DFS visits each reachable cell once (visited set) and records both forward and reverse edge weights (reverse weight is observed when moving back). After mapping, Dijkstra returns the minimum sum of the edge weights (entering costs) to reach the target. If target was never seen, return -1.

Implementation notes
- Represent the unknown grid coordinates by mapping the start to (0,0). Directions map to coordinate deltas.
- Use DFS recursion to explore; when moving into a neighbor, record the edge (current -> neighbor) with the cost returned by master.move. After fully exploring neighbor, move back using the opposite direction, record the reverse edge weight as well.
- After DFS, run standard Dijkstra on the adjacency map.
- Complexity: we visit at most m*n cells (<= 10k); Dijkstra over that graph is O(V log V + E log V) which is fine for constraints.

Rust implementation
- I present an implementation shaped like LeetCode interactive problems. The trait uses a raw-identifier for move (r#move) since move is a Rust keyword.
- The function is Solution::find_shortest_path(master: &dyn GridMaster) -> i32.

Code

```rust
use std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet, BinaryHeap};
use std::cmp::Reverse;

#[allow(unused)]
pub trait GridMaster {
    fn can_move(&self, dir: char) -> bool;
    fn r#move(&self, dir: char) -> i32; // raw identifier because `move` is a keyword
    fn is_target(&self) -> bool;
}

pub struct Solution {}

impl Solution {
    pub fn find_shortest_path(master: &dyn GridMaster) -> i32 {
        // Directions: (char, dx, dy, opposite)
        let dirs: [(char, i32, i32, char); 4] = [
            ('U', -1,  0, 'D'),
            ('D',  1,  0, 'U'),
            ('L',  0, -1, 'R'),
            ('R',  0,  1, 'L'),
        ];

        // adjacency: (x,y) -> Vec<((nx,ny), weight_to_enter_neighbor)>
        let mut adj: HashMap<(i32,i32), Vec<((i32,i32), i32)>> = HashMap::new();
        let mut visited: HashSet<(i32,i32)> = HashSet::new();
        let mut target_pos: Option<(i32,i32)> = None;

        // DFS closure
        fn dfs(
            master: &dyn GridMaster,
            x: i32,
            y: i32,
            visited: &mut HashSet<(i32,i32)>,
            adj: &mut HashMap<(i32,i32), Vec<((i32,i32), i32)>>,
            target_pos: &mut Option<(i32,i32)>,
            dirs: &[(char, i32, i32, char); 4],
        ) {
            visited.insert((x,y));
            if master.is_target() {
                *target_pos = Some((x,y));
            }

            for &(dc, dx, dy, opp) in dirs.iter() {
                let nx = x + dx;
                let ny = y + dy;

                if !visited.contains(&(nx, ny)) && master.can_move(dc) {
                    // move into neighbor
                    let cost_to_neighbor = master.r#move(dc);
                    if cost_to_neighbor >= 0 {
                        // record edge current -> neighbor
                        adj.entry((x,y)).or_default().push(((nx,ny), cost_to_neighbor));
                        // visit neighbor
                        dfs(master, nx, ny, visited, adj, target_pos, dirs);
                        // move back to current
                        let cost_back = master.r#move(opp);
                        if cost_back >= 0 {
                            // record reverse edge neighbor -> current
                            adj.entry((nx,ny)).or_default().push(((x,y), cost_back));
                        } else {
                            // This should not normally happen because can_move(dc) was true,
                            // and the reverse move should be allowed; handle gracefully.
                        }
                    }
                } else if master.can_move(dc) {
                    // neighbor already visited; still may want to record direct edge (optional)
                    // We can obtain the cost to move there and back to record adjacency, but
                    // doing that would move the master away from current and require backtracking here.
                    // To keep moves minimal, we skip re-moving into visited neighbors. The reverse
                    // edge will already have been recorded when neighbor was first explored.
                }
            }
        }

        // Start DFS from (0,0)
        dfs(master, 0, 0, &mut visited, &mut adj, &mut target_pos, &dirs);

        // If target not reachable
        let target = match target_pos {
            Some(t) => t,
            None => return -1,
        };

        // Dijkstra from (0,0) to target
        let mut dist: HashMap<(i32,i32), i32> = HashMap::new();
        let start = (0,0);
        let mut heap: BinaryHeap<(Reverse<i32>, (i32,i32))> = BinaryHeap::new();

        dist.insert(start, 0);
        heap.push((Reverse(0), start));

        while let Some((Reverse(d), node)) = heap.pop() {
            if node == target {
                return d;
            }
            // If we have popped a stale entry
            if let Some(&best) = dist.get(&node) {
                if d != best {
                    continue;
                }
            }
            if let Some(neighbors) = adj.get(&node) {
                for &((nx, ny), w) in neighbors.iter() {
                    let nd = d + w;
                    if dist.get(&(nx,ny)).map_or(true, |&old| nd < old) {
                        dist.insert((nx,ny), nd);
                        heap.push((Reverse(nd), (nx,ny)));
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        -1
    }
}
```

Short practical summary
- Use DFS + backtracking to physically and safely explore the hidden grid and build a graph of reachable cells with accurate "enter" costs.
- Apply Dijkstra on that graph to get the minimum total entering-cost from start to target.
- The approach is robust, minimal in extraneous moves (we only visit each reachable cell once and always backtrack), and directly models the interactive movement/cost semantics.

Executive summary
- Goal: count contiguous subarrays whose element sum equals k.
- Best practical solution: prefix-sum + hash map (counts of prefix sums) — O(n) time, O(n) space. It handles negative numbers and large input sizes efficiently.
- Brute-force (O(n^2)) is simple but too slow for n up to 2×10^4.

Problem breakdown and reasoning
- Let prefixSum[i] be the sum of nums[0..i-1]. A subarray nums[l..r] sums to k iff prefixSum[r+1] - prefixSum[l] = k, i.e. prefixSum[l] = prefixSum[r+1] - k.
- For each position r we want to know how many earlier prefix sums equal prefixSum[r+1] - k.
- Maintain a map from prefix sum value -> count of times it has occurred. Initialize map[0] = 1 because a prefix sum equal to k starting at index 0 should be counted.
- Iterate through array computing running prefix sum. For each new prefix:
  - Add countMap[prefix - k] to answer (if present).
  - Increment countMap[prefix].

Why this is the right choice
- Handles negative and positive numbers (sliding-window/two-pointer won't work with negatives).
- Single pass through array, linear time and memory proportional to distinct prefix sums (≤ n).

Complexity
- Time: O(n)
- Space: O(n) (for the hash map)
- Using long long for sums is safe (nums length ≤ 2e4, |nums[i]| ≤ 1000 => sum magnitude ≤ 2e7); still use 64-bit to be robust.

C++ implementation (clean, production-ready)
- Uses unordered_map<long long, int> for counts.
- Uses reserve and max_load_factor tuning to reduce rehashing for large inputs.
- Includes two example runs in main.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int subarraySum(const vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    long long prefix = 0;
    long long target = k;
    unordered_map<long long, int> count;
    count.reserve(nums.size() * 2);       // reduce rehashing
    count.max_load_factor(0.7f);
    count[0] = 1; // empty prefix sum occurs once

    int result = 0;
    for (int x : nums) {
        prefix += x;
        long long need = prefix - target;
        auto it = count.find(need);
        if (it != count.end()) result += it->second;
        ++count[prefix];
    }
    return result;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> nums1 = {1,1,1};
    int k1 = 2;
    cout << "Example 1: " << subarraySum(nums1, k1) << " (expected 2)\n";

    vector<int> nums2 = {1,2,3};
    int k2 = 3;
    cout << "Example 2: " << subarraySum(nums2, k2) << " (expected 2)\n";

    return 0;
}
```

Edge cases and practical tips
- If nums contains very large integers (beyond given constraints), keep using long long for prefix sums; if integers could exceed 64-bit sums you would need big-integer handling.
- If you expect many distinct prefix sums and want deterministic performance, consider using an ordered map or robin-hood hash map implementation; but unordered_map is typically fine.
- For performance-critical systems: avoid frequent rehashing with reserve() as shown; if thread-safety or parallel processing is required, partitioning is non-trivial because subarrays cross partitions — better to keep single-threaded algorithm for simplicity unless n is very large.

