- Provide a generalized suffix array / suffix automaton solution in Swift.
- Add unit tests and benchmarks comparing the naive and optimized approaches on realistic datasets.

Short answer (what I'll deliver): a compact, robust JavaScript function that sorts the array according to the rules you specified, implemented in a stable way and with explicit tie-breakers.

Assumptions I used (explicit so behavior is predictable)
- "Starting letter" = the first character of the string (case-sensitive). If that character is a digit that makes the title a "number-start" title.
- "Numbers should come before letters" means any title whose first character is a digit is ranked before any title whose first character is a letter. Non-alphanumeric first characters are treated after letters.
- If two titles have the same starting character, we apply the length and ASCII tie-breakers you described.
- Ties that remain identical respect original input order (stable sort).

If you instead intended "starting letter" to mean the first alphabetic character that appears anywhere in the string, tell me and I’ll adapt the comparator accordingly.

Code (paste into Node / browser console):

```javascript
function sortBooks(books) {
  // stable sort: keep original index
  const wrapped = books.map((title, idx) => ({ title, idx }));

  const category = ch => {
    if (!ch) return 3;
    if (/\d/.test(ch)) return 0;           // digits first
    if (/[A-Za-z]/.test(ch)) return 1;     // then letters (case-sensitive)
    return 2;                              // then others
  };

  wrapped.sort((a, b) => {
    const s1 = a.title;
    const s2 = b.title;

    const c1 = s1.charAt(0);
    const c2 = s2.charAt(0);

    const cat1 = category(c1);
    const cat2 = category(c2);
    if (cat1 !== cat2) return cat1 - cat2;

    // same category -> compare starting character code (case-sensitive)
    const code1 = c1 ? c1.charCodeAt(0) : -1;
    const code2 = c2 ? c2.charCodeAt(0) : -1;
    if (code1 !== code2) return code1 - code2;

    // same starting character -> length descending
    if (s1.length !== s2.length) return s2.length - s1.length;

    // same starting char and length -> compare ASCII values descending
    const len = Math.min(s1.length, s2.length);
    for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {
      const diff = s2.charCodeAt(i) - s1.charCodeAt(i); // descending
      if (diff !== 0) return diff;
    }

    // fully identical in characters -> keep original order (stable)
    return a.idx - b.idx;
  });

  return wrapped.map(x => x.title);
}

// Example:
const Books = [
  "3 Little Pigs",
  "Alice in Wonderland",
  "1 Fish 2 Fish Red Fish Blue Fish",
  "1984",
  "Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets"
];

console.log(sortBooks(Books));
```

